[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what was changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe problem:[22m The C code uses a namespace macro [38;5;10mSPX_NAMESPACE(s)[0m which expands [38;5;10minitialize_hash_function[0m to [38;5;10mSPX_initialize_hash_function[0m. The test runner ([38;5;10mrunner/src/main.rs[0m) loads the compiled Rust shared library ([38;5;10mlibblake.so[0m) via [38;5;10mdlsym[0m and looks for the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_initialize_hash_function[0m. The Rust translation had the function implemented internally as [38;5;10minitialize_hash_function[0m in [38;5;10mhash_blake.rs[0m (and it was called by other Rust code like [38;5;10msign.rs[0m), but it was never exported from the cdylib with the namespaced C symbol name.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix:[22m Added a single [38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)] pub extern "C"[0m function to [38;5;10mlib.rs[0m:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10m#[unsafe(no_mangle)]
pub extern "C" fn SPX_initialize_hash_function(_ctx: *mut context::SpxCtx) {
    // no-op for blake
}
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is a no-op, matching the C implementation exactly — for the BLAKE hash variant, [38;5;10minitialize_hash_function[0m does nothing (unlike SHA2 which pre-computes seeded hash states). The translation had the correct logic internally but simply forgot to export the symbol with the [38;5;10mSPX_[0m prefix that the test harness expects.[0m[0m
